### bare semicolon eaten by the shell
find . -name '*.tmp' -exec rm {} ;
>>> SC2067 1:31

### escaped semicolon belongs to the next command
find . -type f -exec touch {} && echo done \;
>>> SC2067 1:28
>>- SC1010 1:39 -- no loop, if or case is open here for this keyword to close, so the shell can only have meant it as a plain word

### plus belongs to grep after a pipe
find . -execdir cat {} | grep needle +
>>> SC2067 1:21

### ok action without a terminator
find /srv -name core -ok rm {}
>>> none

### plus terminator is fine
find . -name '*.sh' -exec chmod 755 {} +
>>> none

### escaped semicolon after a bash script is fine
find . -name '*.md' -execdir bash -c 'test -s "$1" && echo "$1"' _ {} \;
>>> none

### quoted semicolon is fine
find . -name '*.log' -exec gzip {} ';'
>>> none
